# MINIGAME DESIGN RULES

This application contains educational minigames for children.

All minigames must prioritize:
- fun
- clarity
- responsiveness
- dopamine feedback
- replayability
- learning reinforcement

# GAMEPLAY PRINCIPLES

Minigames should:
- feel intuitive
- have immediate feedback
- reward participation
- avoid frustration
- encourage curiosity
- maintain engagement

# GAME FLOW

Every minigame should contain:
- clear objective
- visual onboarding
- rewarding interactions
- progression systems
- completion feedback
- retry encouragement

# EDUCATIONAL PURPOSE

Minigames must reinforce:
- English vocabulary
- pronunciation
- listening
- memory
- association
- fast recognition
- sentence building

# GAME MECHANICS

Prefer:
- drag and drop
- matching systems
- reaction mechanics
- memory games
- quizzes
- puzzle mechanics
- collection systems
- rewards
- XP systems
- stars
- streaks
- achievements

# IMPORTANT

Avoid:
- overly complex mechanics
- confusing interfaces
- punishing systems
- frustrating fail states

# FAIL STATES

Children should never feel punished.

Prefer:
- soft failure
- encouragement
- retries
- positive reinforcement

# PERFORMANCE

Games must:
- feel smooth
- minimize latency
- avoid freezes
- provide instant feedback

# CODE STRUCTURE

Gameplay logic must:
- remain modular
- reusable
- isolated
- maintainable

Avoid duplicated game logic.

# GAME SAFETY

Never break:
- score systems
- rewards
- progression
- save systems
- student progress tracking